WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.110
Good Morning John
At the very first VidCon, I was being interviewed

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:07.149
by a television reporter who, at one point,
was surprised by something I said, and she

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:10.170
said to me
"You can make money on YouTube?

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:10.670
How does that work?

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.160
And I was a little tired.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.120
And I was also, like, super high on VidCon.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.900
Like I was so excited about online video and
all the stuff that had come together

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.480
and so like I did—I did not censor myself
well.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.920
I said something to the effect of
“Um, advertising.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.480
My show has more viewers than yours does.”

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.580
Which I did not intend to be a sick
burn!

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.610
It just came out!

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.250
Like that was the thing that was true.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.890
But the answer to that question is complicated,
and since I think about YouTube a lot, and

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.010
I think that we should all be more open about
how all of these things work, let’s do it,

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.330
let’s talk about how YouTubers make their
money.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.050
Different kinds of content have to have different
kinds of support.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.719
Like this right here, very low budget.

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:47.719
This is just two guys.

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:50.589
We could probably subsist on what we call
“adsense and merch.”

00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:53.710
Adsense works for every YouTuber, as long
as you’re not like philosophically opposed

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:54.710
to advertising.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.559
You click a little box when you upload your
video, YouTube will sell an ad against it,

00:00:57.559 --> 00:01:00.100
and then it will split the revenue with you,
you get 55%.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.100
This is kind of wonderful.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.820
It is not a common thing among social media
sites, though they have started to share some revenue

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.080
YouTube started doing it way back in like
2007 and 2008.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.480
It’s also part of why it’s such an appealing
place to make stuff, and also part of why

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.159
it’s become harder to break in.

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:16.649
Because if there’s a way to make money,
then people will spend money to make their

00:01:16.649 --> 00:01:18.809
content look better so that they can compete
on the platform.

00:01:18.809 --> 00:01:22.780
Anyway, YouTube AdSense pretty much stays
on the same level, and everybody has access

00:01:22.789 --> 00:01:24.289
to that, which is really amazing.

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:27.430
But at higher levels, you start talking about
brand deals, maybe endorsement deals, maybe

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:28.579
really good merch stuff.

00:01:28.579 --> 00:01:32.810
And even eventually getting support from someone
else to help realize your vision.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.530
Crash Course and SciShow were both funded
by grants from YouTube because they wanted

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.950
more high quality stuff on the site so advertisers
would be more likely to advertise.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.469
Those systems are really hard to democratize
and make available to everybody.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.640
Now brand deals are a little bit different.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.320
They have started to be available to creators
sort of in the middle class.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.539
Brand deals, basically, anytime somebody pays
you to talk about something in a video, whether

00:01:53.539 --> 00:01:57.210
that’s like an audible.com callout at the
end, or it can be a big elaborate production

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.750
and your entire video is about the topic that
the brand wants you to talk about.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:04.560
YouTube advertising is available to everybody,
and it comes in and you don’t have to worry

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.009
about whether or not it’s gonna come in,
but it’s a really low rate.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.960
In order to supplement income, a lot of YouTubers,
in addition to having, you know regular jobs,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:18.040
also live in Los Angeles, where it’s easier
to get gigs acting or hosting and TV commercials or shows.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.240
And if you have more social media followers
these days, it can be easier to get that kind

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.830
of work, which is interesting.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:27.250
Some YouTubers also make money by touring,
whether they’re comedians or musicians.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.210
Sometimes creators will license their content
to other platforms.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.050
Sometimes videos go on TV and you make some
money that way.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.830
And then you have some creators like Rooster
Teeth, building their own subscription platforms.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:39.180
And then if you don’t want to make people
subscribe, you can also ask them to subscribe

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.080
through services like Patreon, which is extremely
important for shows like Sexplanations,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.340
for example, that gets really bad YouTube ad rates because

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.520
advertisers don't want to be next to the word "sex."

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.940
Without the support of Patreon patrons, that show couldn’t happen at all.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:56.160
The thing to remember is that different kinds
of content work better with different kinds

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.160
of monetization.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.810
Unlike what I said to that lady at VidCon
2010, it’s not simple.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.670
It’s gotten a lot more difficult to make
your way on this platform, and it has become

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:03.670
more complicated.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.230
Which is one of the reasons why I recently
started a thing called the Internet Creators Guild

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:13.090
with the goal of being a resource to
connect creators together, to give them information,

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:18.360
and to let them have a unified voice when
talking about when things go wrong, and what

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.310
they would like to make them happier.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.020
To be clear, I started this thing, but I really
don’t want to run it.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.600
I don’t think that I should run it, and
I definitely can’t.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.471
Honestly, I think that I’m too biased to
be in charge, so I’m looking forward to

00:03:28.471 --> 00:03:32.031
eventually stepping back after helping Laura
Chernikoff, who is one of the most effective

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.680
people I’ve ever met, get established as
the executive director of the organization.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.540
There is a lot of value that’s created by
online video, and as different systems evolve

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.940
for capturing at least some of that value,
it just gets more complicated.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:48.950
And as those barriers rise, we need to create
systems that keep the benefits of this revolution

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.330
accessible to the most people possible, and
I hope that the Internet Creators Guild

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:53.280
can play a part in that.

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.060
Thanks, everybody, for listening to this.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.890
Hopefully it wasn’t too inside baseball.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:58.160
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

